    },
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p23-q1",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "For $n\\geq 2$ people seated around a circular table, there are $(n-1)!$ distinct circular arrangements.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "True. Rotations represent the same arrangement, so fixing one person leaves $(n-1)!$ arrangements of the others.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 0
      },
      {
        "id": "p23-q2",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "If the table has a marked “head” seat, then there are $(n-1)!$ distinct circular arrangements.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False. There are $n!$ arrangements.",
          "worked": "False. The marked head seat distinguishes the positions, so rotations give different arrangements. The $n$ people can occupy these positions in $n!$ ways.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 0
      },
      {
        "id": "p23-q3",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "With $n$ distinct people, and two specific people required to sit together, the number of circular arrangements is $2(n-1)!$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False. It is $2(n-2)!$.",
          "worked": "False. Treat the two specified people as one block. There are $n-1$ entities to arrange in a circle and two orders within the block, giving $2\\times(n-2)!$ arrangements.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 0
      },
      {
        "id": "p23-q4",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "With $n$ even $(n\\geq 4)$, and two specific people required to sit directly opposite one another, the number of circular arrangements is $(n-2)!$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "True. Fix one specified person. The other has one seat directly opposite. The remaining $n-2$ people can be arranged in $(n-2)!$ ways.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 0
      },
      {
        "id": "p23-q5",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "Requiring that $B$ and $C$ sit immediately next to $A$ (in some order) gives $2(n-3)!$ circular arrangements.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "True. Fix $A$. Its two neighbouring seats can be filled by $B$ and $C$ in two orders. Arrange the remaining $n-3$ people in the remaining seats, giving $2(n-3)!$ arrangements.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 0
      }
    ]
  },
